export default "# About AnimeRec\n\nAnimeRec (anime.abdulhabeeb.com) is a top-10 anime recommender demo. Visitors answer a short onboarding (genres they like, titles they loved, genres to skip, series or movies, how recent), and get ten picks, each with a one-line reason. They can change their picks later with the search button and refine with the plus button (genre, release year, new titles only, sequels).\n\nHow to use the site:\n- The search button (magnifier) adds titles you loved or disliked; the recommendations update straight away.\n- The plus button filters by genre, release year, new titles only, and whether sequels of your picks are included. \"Retake preferences\" starts onboarding again.\n- The rail on the left (or the pill at the bottom on phones) switches between All, TV, Movie, OVA, ONA and Special.\n- \"Love this\" on the featured title adds it to your picks.\n- Answers are saved only in the visitor's browser (local storage and the page URL). Nothing is stored on a server.\n\nThe catalog: 10,720 titles from MyAnimeList, with activity up to February 2018, so anything that first aired after early 2018 is not included. Adult titles and music videos are excluded. Star ratings are MyAnimeList community scores, shown for reference only and never used by the recommender.\n\nBuilt by Abdul Habeeb, a Data Science and Full-Stack Developer and Master's student in Data Science and AI at Saarland University in Saarbrücken, Germany. Portfolio: abdulhabeeb.com. The source code is public on GitHub (Habeeb9731/anime-recommender), and there is a case study on his portfolio.\n\nThis assistant runs on Claude Haiku in a Cloudflare Worker. It suggests titles from its own knowledge, and every suggestion is checked against the AnimeRec catalog before it is shown, so only titles the demo knows appear. Adding them to your picks hands them to the recommender itself, which then ranks the full catalog for you.\n\n# Hybrid Anime Recommender\n\nA top-10 anime recommender that handles brand-new titles and brand-new visitors. It blends collaborative filtering with synopsis and metadata signals, was evaluated with a global time-based split against the full catalog, and ships as an instant Streamlit demo.\n\n**Demo:** [anime.abdulhabeeb.com](https://anime.abdulhabeeb.com)\n\n## Problem\n\nCollaborative filtering cannot rank a title nobody has watched, and popularity lists ignore individual taste. This project ranks every eligible title for each user, shifts smoothly toward content signals as a title's history falls to zero, and folds in new users without retraining.\n\n## Results\n\n\n**The hybrid beats all-time popularity by 312.6% on NDCG@10** (0.1517 vs 0.0368; paired bootstrap 95% CI on the lift +297.7% to +327.0%).\n\nAgainst the harder recent-popularity baseline (0.0929), the lift is 63.3% (95% CI +60.6% to +66.2%), which is also statistically clear.\n\nAgainst EASE alone (0.1521), the hybrid scores 0.1517: a difference of -0.0004 (95% CI -0.0009 to +0.0000), so the two are effectively level overall. The hybrid's purpose is reaching titles EASE cannot score at all, not raising overall accuracy.\n\nCold titles are handled only partly. Ranked among cold titles alone, the hybrid reaches NDCG@10 0.1364 versus 0.0000 for popularity. In full-catalog ranking they take just 0.27% of recommendation slots and NDCG@10 on them is 0.0003, so they rarely reach a user's top 10.\n\n| Model | NDCG@10 | Recall@10 | Precision@10 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Popularity (all-time) | 0.0368 | 0.0362 | 0.0250 |\n| Recent popularity | 0.0929 | 0.0963 | 0.0534 |\n| EASE | 0.1521 | 0.1521 | 0.0874 |\n| ALS | 0.1292 | 0.1300 | 0.0773 |\n| Content | 0.0533 | 0.0537 | 0.0343 |\n| Hybrid | 0.1517 | 0.1524 | 0.0863 |\n\n| Segment | Users | Popularity | Recent popularity | Hybrid |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| All users | 25193 | 0.0368 | 0.0929 | 0.1517 |\n| Users with little history | 101 | 0.1826 | 0.1439 | 0.2013 |\n| Warm titles | 25172 | 0.0368 | 0.0931 | 0.1520 |\n| Cold titles (full-catalog ranking) | 938 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.0003 |\n| Cold titles (ranked among cold only) | 938 | 0.0000 | 0.0000 | 0.1364 |\n\nTest window 2018-02-01 to 2018-05-01, 25,193 users, 10,720 eligible titles (341 cold). Full details are in [reports/results.md](reports/results.md).\n\n\n## Dataset and license\n\n| Source | Used for | License |\n|---|---|---|\n| [MyAnimeList Dataset, azathoth42](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/azathoth42/myanimelist) | Interactions, timestamps, metadata | Open Database License (database), Database Contents License (contents) |\n| [MyAnimeList Dataset, svanoo](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/svanoo/myanimelist-dataset) | Synopses, joined by anime ID | CC0 |\n\nRaw data is not committed; `make data` downloads it from Kaggle's public endpoint without credentials. Usernames are dropped during processing. The ODbL requires attribution and share-alike for derived databases, so check those terms before redistributing the processed data. The demo hotlinks official cover art, which belongs to its rights holders and is not covered by these licenses; review that before deploying publicly, or set `ANIMEREC_SHOW_COVERS=0` for generated art.\n\n## Architecture\n\n\n\n## Methodology\n\n- **Task:** top-K implicit-feedback ranking. A positive is a completed title with a score of at least 7 or above the user's training-period mean. Dropped titles are negative signals.\n- **Splits:** train before 2017-11-01, validation to 2018-02-01, test to 2018-05-01. Tuning used validation only.\n- **Ranking:** every model ranks the full eligible catalog. Titles already in a user's history are excluded, and there is no negative sampling.\n- **Leakage controls:** all behavioral inputs come from before the fit cutoff, eligibility comes from metadata only, and crawl-time aggregates such as global score and members are excluded.\n- **Models:** all-time and recent popularity, EASE, ALS, a content model, and the hybrid.\n- **Hybrid:** per title, `alpha = n / (n + kappa)`, where `n` is the title's training interactions. The score is `alpha * (z(CF) + w * z(recent popularity)) + (1 - alpha) * (z(content) + cold_bias)`.\n- **Uncertainty:** paired bootstrap over users for the NDCG@10 difference and lift.\n\nChoices and trade-offs are recorded in [DECISIONS.md](DECISIONS.md). The full report is [reports/results.md](reports/results.md), and generated resume text is in [reports/claims.md](reports/claims.md).\n\n## Limitations\n\n- Timestamps record the last edit of a list entry, so a title re-edited later is missing from earlier history.\n- The data ends in May 2018, so the demo's \"new\" titles are new as of that snapshot.\n- Results cover a seeded sample of users from a public-list crawl, which may not represent all viewers.\n- Titles with no matched synopsis rely on genre, studio, type and year only.\n- Cold titles rarely reach the top 10 in full-catalog ranking, and shows airing during the test window with little earlier history are among the most-missed titles.\n- The hybrid is level with plain EASE overall; its added value is being able to score titles with no history.\n- Synopses come from a later crawl than the interactions. They describe the title, not its audience, but the text may have been edited after the study window.\n\n# Decisions\n\nChoices made while building this project, and why. Measured values live in `reports/results.md`.\n\n## Data\n\n**Interactions come from `azathoth42/myanimelist`, not the newer `svanoo/myanimelist-dataset`.**\nThe newer crawl looked ideal because it has synopses and a `last_interaction_date`, so I audited that column first. Almost all values equal the title's air date or a single placeholder date, which means they say when a show aired and not when a user acted. A split on it would look temporal without being temporal. `azathoth42` has per-user `my_last_updated` epochs with a natural daily cycle. The audit is rerun by `make data` and reported in `reports/results.md`.\n\n**Synopses are joined from `svanoo` by anime ID.** The interaction source has no synopses. The ID match rate is reported. Titles with no usable synopsis keep a zero text vector and still get genre, studio, type and year features. Those unmatched titles account for a small share of interactions.\n\n**`my_last_updated` is the last edit, not the watch date.** Each user-title pair has one row, so a title re-edited later appears at the later time and is absent from earlier history. This is the main remaining limit of the temporal split.\n\n**User sample: 100,000 of the users left after cleaning, seeded.** This keeps EASE and ALS tuning practical on 16 GB of RAM. All cleaning filters run on the full population before sampling, so the filter log describes the whole dataset.\n\n**Study window ends 2018-05-01.** The crawl tails off during May 2018, so the partial last month is removed.\n\n**Splits.** Train is everything before 2017-11-01, validation is 2017-11-01 to 2018-02-01, and test is 2018-02-01 to 2018-05-01. Each window is three months and spans a change of anime season, so new titles appear in it.\n\n**Bot-like filters.** Four rules: lists over 5,000 titles; at least half of a user's rows in the same second; 50 or more ratings with one distinct score; 100 or more completions in one day. The last rule removes the most data. Most of those accounts are probably people back-filling a list rather than bots, but their timestamps do not reflect viewing order, so they cannot support a temporal evaluation.\n\n**Adult titles and music videos are excluded** from the catalog and from evaluation, because the demo is public and because music videos are not comparable viewing decisions.\n\n**Crawl-time aggregates are not used** (global score, rank, members, favorites). Only allow-listed metadata columns reach the catalog, and a test enforces this. Synopsis text and studio credits come from later crawls, but they describe the title rather than its audience.\n\n## Target and evaluation\n\n- **Label definition is fixed for evaluation** (completed and score at least 7, or above the user's fit-window mean), so all models are compared on the same labels. The training target is tuned separately on validation, and label-threshold sensitivity is reported.\n- **Unrated completions are not positives**, following the brief. They still count as seen, so they are never recommended back.\n- **Plan-to-watch titles do not count as seen**, so they can be recommended.\n- **Eligible catalog comes from metadata only**: titles with a known release date before the end of the evaluation window. Using titles that received interactions in the window would leak the answer.\n- **\"All users\" requires some earlier history**, because no model can personalize without it. Users with no history are reported separately.\n- **Cold-title results appear in two forms**: within the full-catalog ranking, and ranked only among cold titles. Full-catalog is the honest product measure; the restricted form isolates ordering quality.\n- **The test window was evaluated only in the final pipeline run.** All development used validation. `scripts/train.py` removes test-window rows immediately after loading, and `scripts/evaluate.py` refuses to run against a different frozen configuration once the test set has been scored.\n\n## Models\n\n- **EASE was chosen over Item-KNN** because it is closed-form and has one main hyperparameter.\n- **ALS uses `implicit`.** Fold-in is reimplemented in NumPy so the demo does not need that package, and a test checks it against `implicit`.\n- **Popularity baselines count labelled positives** using the fixed label definition, regardless of the target the collaborative models train on.\n- **Hybrid selection uses a constraint.** Cold titles are a small share of positives, so overall NDCG alone would not reward handling them. Among configurations within 1% of the best validation NDCG@10, the one ranking cold titles best is selected.\n- **No two-tower model or LightGBM reranker.** The brief makes these conditional on a clear validation need. The hybrid led every other model on validation, narrowly ahead of EASE, so none was added. On the test set it was level with EASE, as reported in `reports/results.md`.\n\n## Demo\n\n- **Streamlit**, loading a small precomputed bundle. EASE is truncated to the strongest neighbors per title, and the effect of truncation on validation NDCG is reported.\n- **Official covers are shown, at the owner's explicit request.** The original brief allowed images only if permitted. MyAnimeList covers are copyrighted and neither dataset license covers them, so this needs review before any public deployment. Covers are hotlinked and no image files are in the repository. Wide hero banners are hotlinked from AniList's public API on the same basis, added by `scripts/add_banners.py`. `ANIMEREC_SHOW_COVERS=0` switches to generated art.\n- **Community scores appear on cards for display only.** They are loaded while exporting the demo bundle, stay out of the catalog, and never reach a model.\n- **Same-franchise titles are hidden by default**, with a checkbox to allow sequels.\n- **Disliked titles** get negative weight in fold-in and are excluded from results.\n- **Cards open an info popup first**, built from dataset fields added by `scripts/add_details.py`. Details are display-only, and the link to MyAnimeList sits inside the popup.\n- **Onboarding answers persist in the visitor's browser** through local storage and the page URL, with no server-side storage, so a refresh or return visit skips onboarding. **Retake preferences** clears them.\n- **The demo model is the evaluated model**: fit on activity before 2018-02-01. It is not refit on later data, so what it serves matches what was tested.";
